@meta
id: 27-18
title: One key, two jobs
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: mapping, buffer-switch
par: 118
minutes: 8
vscode: a "when" clause in keybindings.json, if you can work out the syntax
open: refund.entity.ts RELEASE.md

@brief
Two buffers, two repetitive jobs.

`refund.entity.ts` is missing a semicolon on each of its five fields.
`RELEASE.md` needs `- ` in front of each of its five notes.

Both are worth a mapping and neither is worth a second key, because you
will never want the semicolon one while you are in the markdown. Add
`<buffer>` to a mapping and it exists only in the buffer you defined it
in — which is how the same key can mean the right thing in every file
type, and how filetype plugins give you per-language keys without
fighting each other for the keyboard.

Use `-` in both buffers. Define it twice, once per buffer.

For the TypeScript one, make it a `:s` command rather than an insert, and
put `<silent>` on it so it does not report a substitution on every line.

@keys
| `:nnoremap <buffer> - …` | a mapping that exists in this buffer only |
| `:nnoremap <silent> - …` | run it without echoing the command or its output |
| `:nmap <buffer>` | list this buffer's normal-mode mappings |
| `:nunmap <buffer> -` | remove this buffer's one, leaving any global one |
| `:map <buffer> <LocalLeader>x` | the usual home for a per-filetype key |

@hints
Nothing stops you binding `-` twice, as long as each one is marked
`<buffer>`. Define the first, use it, switch, define the second.
--
`<buffer>` and `<silent>` go between the command and the key, in either
order, each in angle brackets.
--
`:nnoremap <buffer> <silent> - :s/$/;/<CR>` `Enter`, then `2G` and
`-j` five times. `:bn`, then
`:nnoremap <buffer> - I- <Esc>` `Enter`, `2G` and `-j` five times, `:wqa`.

@solution
    :nnoremap <buffer> <silent> - :s/$/;/<CR><Enter>
    2G  -j-j-j-j-
    :bn<Enter>
    :nnoremap <buffer> - I- <Esc><Enter>
    2G  -j-j-j-j-  :wqa<Enter>

A hundred and three keystrokes, most of them the two definitions.

`<buffer>` is the mapping equivalent of `setlocal`, and it has the same
place in a config: a mapping that only makes sense for one language goes
in `after/ftplugin/<lang>.vim` with `<buffer>` on it, next to the
`setlocal` lines from earlier in this chapter. Vim then installs it on
every buffer of that type and nowhere else, and you never think about it
again.

Buffer-local mappings win over global ones, which is the useful way round.
Map `<leader>t` globally to "run the test file", then give the Python
ftplugin a `<buffer>` version that runs pytest instead, and the key does
the right thing everywhere without a single `if`.

`<silent>` suppresses the echo of a mapping's command line and its output.
Without it, the TypeScript mapping prints a substitution report for every
line you touch, the messages pile up, and eventually vim stops to show you
a `-- More --` prompt in the middle of your edit. Any mapping whose
right-hand side starts with `:` wants `<silent>`. Mappings that stay in
normal mode do not need it.

The other flags you will meet: `<expr>` evaluates the right-hand side as an
expression rather than typing it, `<nowait>` stops vim waiting to see
whether you meant a longer mapping, and `<unique>` refuses to overwrite an
existing binding — which is how a well-behaved plugin avoids stealing a
key you already use.

@start refund.entity.ts
export type Refund = {
  id: string
  orderId: string
  amountPence: number
  reason: string
  createdAt: Date
}

@expected refund.entity.ts
export type Refund = {
  id: string;
  orderId: string;
  amountPence: number;
  reason: string;
  createdAt: Date;
}

@start RELEASE.md
# Release 2026.09.2
refunds outside the window record a reason
the export job streams instead of buffering
customer search includes archived records
webhook signatures are re-read on rotate
invoice totals round half up

@expected RELEASE.md
# Release 2026.09.2
- refunds outside the window record a reason
- the export job streams instead of buffering
- customer search includes archived records
- webhook signatures are re-read on rotate
- invoice totals round half up
